{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551938-what-books-are-essential-for-wta","id":1551938,"title":"What books are essential for WtA","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-06-30T05:56:51-07:00","id":1551938,"title":"What books are essential for WtA","content":"So I was looking and my library of WtA is rather small, being a player most of my times in Werewolf I never picked up any sourcebooks. Now I see myself diving back into it and want to switch roles. So as a Storyteller what books do you all see as key essentials to have in your library?<br /> <br /> Thanks","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1680-justicar-seth","name":"Justicar-Seth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1680_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-30T10:22:16-07:00","id":1551944,"title":"","content":"Besides the corebook, I find these to be critical to have:<br /> <br /> The Players Guide (I think the 2e one is the strongest, but any edition is good)<br /> Umbra: the Velvet Shadow (I slightly prefer the 1e version, but again any edition tends to be strong)<br /> Book of the Wyrm (any edition)<br /> Axis Mundi: the Book of Spirits<br /> Storyteller's Handbook (1e only)<br /> Book of the City<br /> <br /> The above should provide a solid basis for any chronicle. The contain critical information, have lots of good details and examples, and are high quality.<br /> <br /> If you want to go beyond those, I also recommend Ways of the Wolf, Book of the Weaver, and Book of the Wyld. I'd also recommend picking up all the editions of the Players Guide because each one has information not found in the others (like certain rites, fetishes, totems, sept information, and so on), but of course 80% of it will be duplicate information. So your mileage may vary. However, I find myself pulling stuff from each players guide. I don't know if Caerns: Places of Power is worth it. As example caerns for each of the tribes, it is just OK. However, it's brief four pages talking about moon bridges contains information not incorporated into any other sourcebook that I can recall. (The game really would have benefited from incorporating things like this into future versions of the Players Guide or Storyteller's Handbook. There are lots of pieces of &quot;abandoned lore&quot; like the concept of Lunatics in the Valkenberg Foundation that should have been reworked into the essential sourcebooks rather than left alone.)<br /> <br /> Concerning the various tribebooks and breedbooks, it really depends how deep you want to explore and focus on any individual tribes or breeds for your chronicle. Usually between the corebook and the Players Guide, you have more than enough. However, if you really want to go into the specifics of a certain tribe or utilize a certain Changing Breed heavily in your chronicle, it may be worth picking up. The quality of the sourcebooks greatly varies from book to book, ranging all the way from excellent to awful.<br /> <br /> I am also not that impressed with any of the Rage regional sourcebooks. Some are stronger than others and contain some neat information found only there, but overall they tend to be mediocre. However, having one on hand as an example of what a setting &quot;should&quot; be like (in terms of this is how the game's creators thought the setting should be like) can be useful to a new ST. It is not intuitive that a Werewolf game setting should cover multiple caerns organized in a system of geographical protectorates, allowing opportunities for anruth packs to travel and inter-sept politics. (Most chronicles tend to include one huge multi-tribal sept and focus everything there.) If that would be useful to you, then I think Rage Across New York would be the best for that purpose. Just expect it to have various flaws.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-30T14:33:49-07:00","id":1551960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551938-what-books-are-essential-for-wta?p=1551944#post1551944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides the corebook, I find these to be critical to have:<br /> <br /> The Players Guide (I think the 2e one is the strongest, but any edition is good)<br /> Umbra: the Velvet Shadow (I slightly prefer the 1e version, but again any edition tends to be strong)<br /> Book of the Wyrm (any edition)<br /> Axis Mundi: the Book of Spirits<br /> Storyteller's Handbook (1e only)<br /> Book of the City<br /> <br /> The above should provide a solid basis for any chronicle. The contain critical information, have lots of good details and examples, and are high quality.<br /> <br /> If you want to go beyond those, I also recommend Ways of the Wolf, Book of the Weaver, and Book of the Wyld. I'd also recommend picking up all the editions of the Players Guide because each one has information not found in the others (like certain rites, fetishes, totems, sept information, and so on), but of course 80% of it will be duplicate information. So your mileage may vary. However, I find myself pulling stuff from each players guide. I don't know if Caerns: Places of Power is worth it. As example caerns for each of the tribes, it is just OK. However, it's brief four pages talking about moon bridges contains information not incorporated into any other sourcebook that I can recall. (The game really would have benefited from incorporating things like this into future versions of the Players Guide or Storyteller's Handbook. There are lots of pieces of &quot;abandoned lore&quot; like the concept of Lunatics in the Valkenberg Foundation that should have been reworked into the essential sourcebooks rather than left alone.)<br /> <br /> Concerning the various tribebooks and breedbooks, it really depends how deep you want to explore and focus on any individual tribes or breeds for your chronicle. Usually between the corebook and the Players Guide, you have more than enough. However, if you really want to go into the specifics of a certain tribe or utilize a certain Changing Breed heavily in your chronicle, it may be worth picking up. The quality of the sourcebooks greatly varies from book to book, ranging all the way from excellent to awful.<br /> <br /> I am also not that impressed with any of the Rage regional sourcebooks. Some are stronger than others and contain some neat information found only there, but overall they tend to be mediocre. However, having one on hand as an example of what a setting &quot;should&quot; be like (in terms of this is how the game's creators thought the setting should be like) can be useful to a new ST. It is not intuitive that a Werewolf game setting should cover multiple caerns organized in a system of geographical protectorates, allowing opportunities for anruth packs to travel and inter-sept politics. (Most chronicles tend to include one huge multi-tribal sept and focus everything there.) If that would be useful to you, then I think Rage Across New York would be the best for that purpose. Just expect it to have various flaws.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks you for this very detailed info. Much appreciate it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1680-justicar-seth","name":"Justicar-Seth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1680_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-01T07:40:45-07:00","id":1551973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551938-what-books-are-essential-for-wta?p=1551944#post1551944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides the corebook, I find these to be critical to have:<br /> <br /> The Players Guide (I think the 2e one is the strongest, but any edition is good)<br /> Umbra: the Velvet Shadow (I slightly prefer the 1e version, but again any edition tends to be strong)<br /> Book of the Wyrm (any edition)<br /> Axis Mundi: the Book of Spirits<br /> Storyteller's Handbook (1e only)<br /> Book of the City<br /> <br /> The above should provide a solid basis for any chronicle. The contain critical information, have lots of good details and examples, and are high quality.<br /> <br /> If you want to go beyond those, I also recommend Ways of the Wolf, Book of the Weaver, and Book of the Wyld. I'd also recommend picking up all the editions of the Players Guide because each one has information not found in the others (like certain rites, fetishes, totems, sept information, and so on), but of course 80% of it will be duplicate information. So your mileage may vary. However, I find myself pulling stuff from each players guide. I don't know if Caerns: Places of Power is worth it. As example caerns for each of the tribes, it is just OK. However, it's brief four pages talking about moon bridges contains information not incorporated into any other sourcebook that I can recall. (The game really would have benefited from incorporating things like this into future versions of the Players Guide or Storyteller's Handbook. There are lots of pieces of &quot;abandoned lore&quot; like the concept of Lunatics in the Valkenberg Foundation that should have been reworked into the essential sourcebooks rather than left alone.)<br /> <br /> Concerning the various tribebooks and breedbooks, it really depends how deep you want to explore and focus on any individual tribes or breeds for your chronicle. Usually between the corebook and the Players Guide, you have more than enough. However, if you really want to go into the specifics of a certain tribe or utilize a certain Changing Breed heavily in your chronicle, it may be worth picking up. The quality of the sourcebooks greatly varies from book to book, ranging all the way from excellent to awful.<br /> <br /> I am also not that impressed with any of the Rage regional sourcebooks. Some are stronger than others and contain some neat information found only there, but overall they tend to be mediocre. However, having one on hand as an example of what a setting &quot;should&quot; be like (in terms of this is how the game's creators thought the setting should be like) can be useful to a new ST. It is not intuitive that a Werewolf game setting should cover multiple caerns organized in a system of geographical protectorates, allowing opportunities for anruth packs to travel and inter-sept politics. (Most chronicles tend to include one huge multi-tribal sept and focus everything there.) If that would be useful to you, then I think Rage Across New York would be the best for that purpose. Just expect it to have various flaws.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Great list, I would only add <b>Hammer &amp; Klaive</b>, more for the lenghtly list of fetishes in a single place than the crafting mechanics, that would give some variety to the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-01T14:06:47-07:00","id":1551979,"title":"","content":"Agree with Black Fox on the book list. As an additional tip if you get the PDFs, copy &amp; paste the Gift and Rite descriptions each PC has (individually) into a sheet, print and hand them to their players.<br /> 30 minutes of prep that will save hours of flipping through the books.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\